{"data":{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2610.25860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2610.25860","prefix":"10.48550","suffix":"arxiv.2610.25860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2610.25860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"alternateIdentifiers":[{"alternateIdentifierType":"arXiv","alternateIdentifier":"2610.25860"}],"creators":[{"name":"Li, Zhaonan","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaonan","familyName":"Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Cheng, Zelei","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelei","familyName":"Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Mishra, Amritansh","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amritansh","familyName":"Mishra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Lee, Chia-Hsuan","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hsuan","familyName":"Lee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Patel, Mann","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mann","familyName":"Patel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Sahu, Sambit","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sambit","familyName":"Sahu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Zhou, Ben","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"name":"Campbell, William M.","nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William M.","familyName":"Campbell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Decoding Methods for Token-Efficient Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"lang":"en","subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"lang":"en","subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-10-19T06:13:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:53:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"ris":"GEN","bibtex":"misc","citeproc":"article","schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceType":"Article","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0","rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX"}],"descriptions":[{"description":"Rollout decoding affects both which trajectories are used for reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) and how many completion tokens are spent collecting them. We study whether candidate-token support should remain fixed or widen during training in the context of rollout sampling efficiency. Our main schedule moves from top-K=10 to K=20 and then to full support as the attempted-token budget increases. In a 4B code-RLVR setting, the schedule has a higher interpolated 150M-token validation value than its matched fixed-K=10 continuation in all three runs, with a mean gain of $1.80{\\pm}0.42$ points. The schedule also performs comparably to a separately trained fixed-K=20 baseline, the strongest fixed-support setting in our sweep. Follow-up experiments suggest that late widening to full support is the most promising variant, although the evidence remains noisy, and mixed-reward-group diagnostics do not clearly explain the gain. An exploratory single-run test replay yields identical point estimates for the schedule and fixed $K=10$. Overall, our results show that rollout support is an important control for sampling efficiency in RLVR and motivate further study of adaptive support schedules.","descriptionType":"Abstract"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"xml":"","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","is